{"authored" [{"question" "q-84842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/7cb71517fe60c9d15b6cacddbf5f91c2e4a60103b8917a4d9baa287517a5fd54.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "621c5cdb9a01fa161f399def28bf3e0785424b376982f32b691cf25ca0d97252" "basis" 1479 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-3,-1),$ and passes through the point $(-5,1).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-3,-1),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+3=p{t}^{2},\\,y+1=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,1),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=1$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 2=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+3=(-\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\\,y+1=2(-\\frac{1}{2})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "621c5cdb9a01fa161f399def28bf3e0785424b376982f32b691cf25ca0d97252" "basis" 1479 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "79845caf2b8c39821d2d38f4496d4a43cd5da8ec6a9beaaf51ae2b8fef8bedb7" "basis" 1479 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "621c5cdb9a01fa161f399def28bf3e0785424b376982f32b691cf25ca0d97252" "basis" 1479 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+3=-2{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-4t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "621c5cdb9a01fa161f399def28bf3e0785424b376982f32b691cf25ca0d97252" "basis" 1479 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "621c5cdb9a01fa161f399def28bf3e0785424b376982f32b691cf25ca0d97252" "basis" 1479 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "621c5cdb9a01fa161f399def28bf3e0785424b376982f32b691cf25ca0d97252" "basis" 1479 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{1}{2}t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "621c5cdb9a01fa161f399def28bf3e0785424b376982f32b691cf25ca0d97252" "basis" 1479 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "621c5cdb9a01fa161f399def28bf3e0785424b376982f32b691cf25ca0d97252" "basis" 1479 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-84842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "621c5cdb9a01fa161f399def28bf3e0785424b376982f32b691cf25ca0d97252" "basis" 1479 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/assets/e5c7f025b6f12599a4ecb5fa0cc9e657fd0b67deb1e50231673113f337ca7776.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "621c5cdb9a01fa161f399def28bf3e0785424b376982f32b691cf25ca0d97252" "basis" 1479 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(4,4),$ and passes through the point $(8,8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(4,4),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-4=p{t}^{2},\\,y-4=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(8,8),$ we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=8$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 4=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-4=(1){t}^{2},\\,y-4=2(1)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-4={t}^{2},\\,y-4=2t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "621c5cdb9a01fa161f399def28bf3e0785424b376982f32b691cf25ca0d97252" "basis" 1479 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "79845caf2b8c39821d2d38f4496d4a43cd5da8ec6a9beaaf51ae2b8fef8bedb7" "basis" 1479 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "621c5cdb9a01fa161f399def28bf3e0785424b376982f32b691cf25ca0d97252" "basis" 1479 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+4=2t,\\,\\,y+4={t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "621c5cdb9a01fa161f399def28bf3e0785424b376982f32b691cf25ca0d97252" "basis" 1479 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+4={t}^{2},\\,\\,y+4=2t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "621c5cdb9a01fa161f399def28bf3e0785424b376982f32b691cf25ca0d97252" "basis" 1479 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-4=2t,\\,\\,y-4={t}^{2}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "621c5cdb9a01fa161f399def28bf3e0785424b376982f32b691cf25ca0d97252" "basis" 1479 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-4=2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-4=4t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "621c5cdb9a01fa161f399def28bf3e0785424b376982f32b691cf25ca0d97252" "basis" 1479 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-4={t}^{2},\\,\\,y-4=2t"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "621c5cdb9a01fa161f399def28bf3e0785424b376982f32b691cf25ca0d97252" "basis" 1479 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-4={t}^{2},\\,\\,y-4=2t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/state.json" "file_sha256" "621c5cdb9a01fa161f399def28bf3e0785424b376982f32b691cf25ca0d97252" "basis" 1479 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041015/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-84842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/assets/7cb71517fe60c9d15b6cacddbf5f91c2e4a60103b8917a4d9baa287517a5fd54.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "668ea37a9be850216f63f73becc525bd56ba2f77e2f11174958535488cb618d5"} {"question" "q-84842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(-3,-1),$ and passes through the point $(-5,1).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(-3,-1),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx+3=p{t}^{2},\\,y+1=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(-5,1),$ we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=1$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 2=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =2pt \\\\ 1 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{1}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2 & =p{(\\frac{1}{p})}^{2} \\\\ -2 & =p\\cdot \\frac{1}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ -2 & =\\frac{1}{p} \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx+3=(-\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\\,y+1=2(-\\frac{1}{2})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "668ea37a9be850216f63f73becc525bd56ba2f77e2f11174958535488cb618d5"} {"question" "q-84842" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4da6a88aaf0039840c39c83a1caea296e0c6885740bb22ae8b811407799b1e60"} {"question" "q-84842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "668ea37a9be850216f63f73becc525bd56ba2f77e2f11174958535488cb618d5"} {"question" "q-84842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+3=-2{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-4t"] ["math" "x+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-\\frac{1}{2}t"] ["math" "x+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-t"] ["math" "x-3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{1}{2}t"] ["math" "x-3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "668ea37a9be850216f63f73becc525bd56ba2f77e2f11174958535488cb618d5"} {"question" "q-84842" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x+3=-\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=-t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "668ea37a9be850216f63f73becc525bd56ba2f77e2f11174958535488cb618d5"} {"question" "q-84851" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+3=2t,\\,y-2={t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "668ea37a9be850216f63f73becc525bd56ba2f77e2f11174958535488cb618d5"} {"question" "q-84851" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x+3}{2},\\,{t}^{2}=y-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x+3}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=y-2$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{4} & =y-2 \\\\ (x+3)^{2} & =4(y-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "668ea37a9be850216f63f73becc525bd56ba2f77e2f11174958535488cb618d5"} {"question" "q-84851" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4da6a88aaf0039840c39c83a1caea296e0c6885740bb22ae8b811407799b1e60"} {"question" "q-84851" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "668ea37a9be850216f63f73becc525bd56ba2f77e2f11174958535488cb618d5"} {"question" "q-84851" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x+3)^{2}=2(y-2)"] ["math" "(x+3)^{2}=4(y-2)"] ["math" "(x+3)^{2}=8(y-2)"] ["math" "(x-3)^{2}=2(y+2)"] ["math" "(x-3)^{2}=4(y+2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "668ea37a9be850216f63f73becc525bd56ba2f77e2f11174958535488cb618d5"} {"question" "q-84851" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x+3)^{2}=4(y-2)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "668ea37a9be850216f63f73becc525bd56ba2f77e2f11174958535488cb618d5"} {"question" "q-84859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx+1=-\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{5}{2}t,\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "668ea37a9be850216f63f73becc525bd56ba2f77e2f11174958535488cb618d5"} {"question" "q-84859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & =-\\frac{4(x+1)}{5},\\,t=-\\frac{2(y-1)}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{2(y-1)}{5})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4(y-1)^{2}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{4(x+1)}{5}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4(y-1)^{2}}{25} & =-\\frac{4(x+1)}{5} \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}\\cdot (-\\frac{4(x+1)}{5}) \\\\ (y-1)^{2} & =-5(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "668ea37a9be850216f63f73becc525bd56ba2f77e2f11174958535488cb618d5"} {"question" "q-84859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4da6a88aaf0039840c39c83a1caea296e0c6885740bb22ae8b811407799b1e60"} {"question" "q-84859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "668ea37a9be850216f63f73becc525bd56ba2f77e2f11174958535488cb618d5"} {"question" "q-84859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(y+1)^{2}=-5(x-1)"] ["math" "(y+1)^{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}(x-1)"] ["math" "(y-1)^{2}=-5(x+1)"] ["math" "(y-1)^{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}(x-1)"] ["math" "(y-1)^{2}=\\frac{5}{2}(x+1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "668ea37a9be850216f63f73becc525bd56ba2f77e2f11174958535488cb618d5"} {"question" "q-84859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(y-1)^{2}=-5(x+1)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "668ea37a9be850216f63f73becc525bd56ba2f77e2f11174958535488cb618d5"} {"question" "q-106461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/assets/e5c7f025b6f12599a4ecb5fa0cc9e657fd0b67deb1e50231673113f337ca7776.png)\n\nWhich of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the parabola shown above?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "668ea37a9be850216f63f73becc525bd56ba2f77e2f11174958535488cb618d5"} {"question" "q-106461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The diagram shows that the parabola is horizontal, has its vertex at $(4,4),$ and passes through the point $(8,8).$\n\nThe parametric equations of a horizontal parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nIn our case, $(h,k)=(4,4),$ and so we have\n\n$$\nx-4=p{t}^{2},\\,y-4=2pt.\n$$\n\nSince the parabola passes through $(8,8),$ we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=8$ into the parametric equations to obtain a system of equations. This gives\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4=p{t}^{2} \\\\ 4=2pt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $t,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2pt \\\\ 2 & =pt \\\\ t & =\\frac{2}{p}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation and solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =p{(\\frac{2}{p})}^{2} \\\\ 4 & =p\\cdot \\frac{4}{{p}^{2}} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{4}{p} \\\\ p & =\\frac{4}{4} \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-4=(1){t}^{2},\\,y-4=2(1)t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-4={t}^{2},\\,y-4=2t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "668ea37a9be850216f63f73becc525bd56ba2f77e2f11174958535488cb618d5"} {"question" "q-106461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4da6a88aaf0039840c39c83a1caea296e0c6885740bb22ae8b811407799b1e60"} {"question" "q-106461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "668ea37a9be850216f63f73becc525bd56ba2f77e2f11174958535488cb618d5"} {"question" "q-106461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+4=2t,\\,\\,y+4={t}^{2}"] ["math" "x+4={t}^{2},\\,\\,y+4=2t"] ["math" "x-4=2t,\\,\\,y-4={t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-4=2{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-4=4t"] ["math" "x-4={t}^{2},\\,\\,y-4=2t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "668ea37a9be850216f63f73becc525bd56ba2f77e2f11174958535488cb618d5"} {"question" "q-106461" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-4={t}^{2},\\,\\,y-4=2t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "668ea37a9be850216f63f73becc525bd56ba2f77e2f11174958535488cb618d5"} {"question" "q-323209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola\n\n$$\nx+7=-3t,\\,y-2=-9{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "668ea37a9be850216f63f73becc525bd56ba2f77e2f11174958535488cb618d5"} {"question" "q-323209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x+7}{3},\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{9}(y-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{x+7}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+7)^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{9}(y-2)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+7)^{2}}{9} & =-\\frac{1}{9}(y-2) \\\\ {(x+7)}^{2} & =-(y-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "668ea37a9be850216f63f73becc525bd56ba2f77e2f11174958535488cb618d5"} {"question" "q-323209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4da6a88aaf0039840c39c83a1caea296e0c6885740bb22ae8b811407799b1e60"} {"question" "q-323209" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "668ea37a9be850216f63f73becc525bd56ba2f77e2f11174958535488cb618d5"} {"question" "q-323209" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "valu